## ===========================================================================
## THE DISGUISE — the dress and hat, the change behind the stall, the boots,
## and the way out
##
## The escape's shape (change document, "The escape disguise", with its
## 2026-08-23/24 amendments): necklace → Teisha → the dress and the fashionable
## hat into the satchel → back up the post → slide on the gray cloak → let go
## mid-wire → Behind Fruit Stall → change → out to the Fruit Stall → east.
##
## THE LOOK IS A STATE, NOT A SET OF OBJECTS (David, 2026-08-24: "all clothing
## is scenery and not directly removable"). Jack carries `states: urchin,
## dressed, identified` in `secret-letter.story`. The dress and hat are items
## only until the change; CHANGE OUTFIT consumes them into the look — they
## leave play, the cap goes into the satchel, and Jack is `dressed`. The
## gray cloak already lies wherever the slide left it. Nothing is worn or
## taken off piece by piece.
##
## THE WINDOWS, in turns (change document, "Both timing questions"):
##
##   1. `let go` mid-wire drops her Behind Fruit Stall and starts the
##      player's `lingering` clock (one named turn, `changing`).
##   2. The next command is the change. If she is STILL behind the stall on
##      the turn after that, `lingering` expires there: the mercenaries move
##      to the Fruit Stall and stepping out is death (the clause lives on
##      Behind Fruit Stall in `aerial-runway.chord`).
##   3. Coming out to the Fruit Stall while `dressed` starts the player's
##      `exposure` clock (no named turns — it expires on the following
##      turn). Leave the stall on that turn and the head start holds; stay,
##      and the boots-spotted beat fires: Jack becomes `identified`, the
##      mercenaries arrive knowing what she wears, and the sweep reads her
##      again.
##
## RECOGNITION (change document, "The recognition rule, sharpened"): the sweep
## reads Jack in every state except `dressed`. `after going while hunted` in
## Jack's block restarts `waiting` only while she is not `dressed`; once
## `identified` it reads her again. No suppression is keyed to the cap.
##
## The east exit is HELD for David beyond the placeholders below: the
## source's Eastern Junction rules (`story.ni:2481-2496`) are the cap-off-era
## captain text the recognition ruling supersedes, kept aside in the comment
## at the bottom of this file.
##
## Every finished line below is Gentry's; every PLACEHOLDER is David's.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE GARMENTS — Teisha's, until the trade (`npc-teisha.chord` gives them)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## DAVID: the dress replaces the source's green silk cloak (`story.ni:3061`,
## "a simple drape of soft, dusky green with silver trim and a voluminous
## hood"); no dress text exists in 2009. Name, colour, and description are
## yours — the noun `dress` and the aliases below are structural stand-ins.

create the dress
  aka gown, green dress, new dress

  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The dress Teisha trades for the necklace.)

  on the player wearing
    refuse change-outfit-instead
  end on

## DAVID: the fashionable hat is the remake's own object. `hat` alone also
## names the woolen cap, so the parser will ask which while both exist.

create the fashionable hat
  aka fashionable hat, new hat, lady's hat, bonnet

  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The fashionable hat that comes with the dress.)

  on the player wearing
    refuse change-outfit-instead
  end on

## Wearing either garment directly is redirected to the outfit change (the
## clothing-is-the-look ruling). Line is David's.

define phrase change-outfit-instead
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Not piece by piece — CHANGE OUTFIT.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CHANGE — one action, one turn, Behind Fruit Stall only
##
## Grammar carries David's three phrasings (CHANGE OUTFIT, SWITCH HATS, WEAR
## DRESS) plus the plain ones a player types. The words are LITERAL: in a
## grammar line `the <word>` declares a slot (`kick the target`), so `wear
## the dress` would have bound any reachable thing as "the dress" and made
## the body's `the dress` a slot reference (unmet on `change outfit` —
## ADR-275 D6). `wear dress` is also stdlib `wearing`'s shape; whether the
## story grammar wins is measured at play time (GH #317's scoping question)
## — the intercepts above catch the case where stdlib wins.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action changing-outfit
  grammar
    change outfit
    change clothes
    switch hats
    put on dress
    wear dress
  the player must have the dress: change-no-dress
  refuse when the player is not in Behind Fruit Stall: change-not-here
  refuse when Jack is not urchin: change-already
  phrase change-outfit
  change Jack to dressed
  remove the dress
  remove the fashionable hat
  move the woolen cap to the cloth satchel

## DAVID: the change itself — the one turn behind the stall. The source's
## nearest line is the cloak's (`story.ni:3087`): "You slip the silk cloak
## over your shoulders and pull the hood up. The fabric feels smooth and
## cool on your skin, but you don't have time to admire it now — you've got
## to run!" — kept aside; the dress and hat need their own.

define phrase change-outfit
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Jack changes into the dress and the fashionable
  hat behind the stall; the cap goes into the satchel.)
end phrase

define phrase change-no-dress
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Nothing to change into yet.)
end phrase

define phrase change-not-here
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Not out here, in the open.)
end phrase

define phrase change-already
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Already changed.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CLOCKS
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## One turn to change, one turn to return (change document, amended
## 2026-08-23). Started by `let go` / `drop` in `aerial-runway.chord`.

define timer lingering for the player
  changing
end timer

## One turn at the Fruit Stall before identification (change document, "Both
## timing questions", 1). Started by the Fruit Stall's own `after the player
## entering while Jack is dressed` clause (`grubbers-market.chord`) — on the
## room itself, not a composed trait: a trait's `entering` clause did not
## fire for the room that carried it (2026-08-29).

define timer exposure for the player
end timer

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE BOOTS — the thread the parchment never mentioned
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## The identification beat itself is a `when the player's exposure expires`
## clause on the mercenaries' own block (`mercenaries.chord`), beside the
## rest of the sweep's clocks; only its line lives here.

## DAVID: the boots-spotted beat — a mercenary notices an urchin's boots
## under a lady's dress; the chase is back on, and now they know what she is
## wearing. No source text (the 2009 game has no footwear).

define phrase boots-spotted
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. A mercenary notices the boots under the dress.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE WAY OUT — Commerce Street
##
## The chapter's end room. Its arrival line in 2009 (`story.ni:4266`) is
## Gentry's "You made it." block — kept aside below because its last
## sentence removes the cloak, and this Jack is in a dress. What happens on
## arrival beyond the text (a chapter break? `win`?) is David's call and is
## not wired.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create Commerce Street
  a room
  aka commerce, the street
  west to the Eastern Junction
  first time
    (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. "You made it." — Gentry's arrival block is
    kept aside in this file's comments; it ends by removing the cloak.)

  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Commerce Street, the chapter's way out.)

  after the player entering while the player's market-escape has started
    reset the player's market-escape
  end after

## Gentry's arrival block (`story.ni:4266`), verbatim, for David to rework:
##   "You made it.
##   You let out a long, shaky breath. You're safe now. Commerce Street is
##   home to a slightly more upscale class of merchant, and business here is
##   not quite as frantic as in Grubber's Market. Transactions tend to be
##   calmer and more deliberate, and involve significantly more money. The
##   Lord's Guard will be far less tolerant of a pack of armed bullies
##   menacing the citizens of the upper class, so it's unlikely that the
##   mercenaries will follow you here. And even if they did, there are too
##   many streets and side alleys for them to block off your escape.
##   Gratefully, you remove the cloak, which was beginning to stick to your
##   skin with all the sweat. Now it's time to pause, catch your breath, and
##   try to figure out why they were after you in the first place."

## The Eastern Junction's east exit (its blocked lines live on the junction
## in `grubbers-market.chord`): deflected while calm, watched while hunted
## and undisguised, open once she is dressed. Both refusal lines are David's;
## Gentry's captain text (`story.ni:2481-2496`) is cap-off-era and is kept
## aside here, not carried:
##   "Just as you reach the edge of the market square, you notice the
##   mercenary captain standing watch at the entrance to Commerce Street.
##   His eyes fall on you before you can duck out of the way, and the next
##   moment you are sprinting back into the market with the captain hot on
##   your heels. You manage to lose him in the crowd... barely."
##   "The mercenary captain is too sharp; you'll need a disguise if you're
##   to have any chance of sneaking past him."

define phrase east-deflected
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The calm walk does not leave the market east.)
end phrase

define phrase east-watched
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The captain watches the Commerce Street
  entrance; not as Jack looks now.)
end phrase
